"""Original October 9 decision briefs. Examples are hypothetical, not client results."""
# slug, title, introduction, A, B, dimensions, scenario, procedure, questions
COMPARISONS = [
('refinish-vs-replace-rental-furniture', 'Refinish vs replace furniture in a vacation rental',
 'An existing piece earns its place by fitting the room and remaining serviceable. Compare restoration with replacement only after inspecting structure, dimensions and the finish. A cosmetic refresh cannot resolve loose joints or a poor layout.', 'Refinish existing furniture', 'Buy a replacement', [
 ('Condition', 'Works best when the frame, joints and hardware are sound and the problem is cosmetic.', 'Useful when structure, dimensions or missing components make restoration impractical.'),
 ('Schedule', 'Requires removal, preparation, drying or curing and transport back to the property.', 'Depends on actual stock, delivery appointment, inspection and assembly.'),
 ('Repeat repairs', 'Keep the finish specification and spare hardware for future touch-ups.', 'Check availability of replacement legs, drawers, covers or other vulnerable parts.')],
 'A hypothetical cabin has a solid dresser with a worn top and drawers that still run smoothly. A refinishing quote may preserve a piece that fits a shallow alcove. The same quote would be less compelling for a dresser with damaged runners, persistent odors and a footprint that blocks the bedroom door. Inspect before deciding which case you have.',
 ['Photograph the piece from every side, measure it and list all defects. Ask a repair specialist which defects are repairable and whether old coatings require specialist handling.', 'Obtain a written restoration scope including collection, materials, finish, curing time and return delivery. Compare it with a replacement delivered and assembled in the same room.', 'Check the calendar for a genuine work window. Keep a substitute if guests need the furniture before the restored piece is ready.', 'Save finish and hardware details in the property inventory. Inspect the piece again after installation rather than accepting a surface-only photo.'],
 ['Does the repair address structure as well as appearance?', 'When can the finish be cleaned and put into guest use?', 'Who pays if transport causes additional damage?']),
('slipcovered-vs-fixed-upholstery-sofa', 'Slipcovered vs fixed upholstery sofas for Airbnb',
 'Compare the complete cleaning cycle, not just whether a cover can come off. A removable cover is helpful only when the care instructions, laundry capacity and spare inventory fit the turnover schedule.', 'Removable slipcover sofa', 'Fixed upholstery sofa', [
 ('Cleaning', 'Verify whether covers are machine washable, dry-clean only or subject to size limits.', 'Follow the specific cleaning code and arrange suitable spot and deep cleaning.'),
 ('Turnover', 'A spare cover can keep the room usable while the dirty cover is cleaned.', 'Wet cleaning may require drying time before the sofa can be used.'),
 ('Replacement', 'Confirm covers are sold separately for the exact model and revision.', 'Ask whether individual cushions, fabric panels or repair services are available.')],
 'A hypothetical beach rental has same-day changeovers and a small laundry closet. The host likes a washable sofa but discovers its main cover needs a larger machine. The workable plan may be a spare cover plus off-site laundry, or a different sofa with smaller removable sections. The label alone does not decide the outcome.',
 ['Request the actual care label before purchase. Check every separate component; cushion covers and the main body may have different instructions.', 'Measure laundry capacity and document who removes, washes, dries and reinstalls the cover. Include transport when cleaning happens off-site.', 'Test the replacement process before launch. Mark the orientation discreetly and photograph a correctly fitted cover for the cleaner.', 'Budget for the spare before comparing prices. Store it dry and labeled, and keep a process for retiring covers that no longer fit after cleaning.'],
 ['Can replacement covers be ordered independently?', 'What is the realistic elapsed cleaning and drying time?', 'Does the warranty cover the intended rental use?']),
('extendable-vs-fixed-dining-table', 'Extendable vs fixed dining tables for vacation rentals',
 'Dining capacity should match how guests actually use the property. Draw the room with chairs occupied, doors moving and any table leaf installed. An advertised seat count does not prove that the room can accommodate it.', 'Extendable dining table', 'Fixed dining table', [
 ('Space', 'Can reduce the everyday footprint but needs room at its fully extended size.', 'Occupies a predictable footprint in every configuration.'),
 ('Storage', 'Loose leaves need a dry, protected location that guests can access safely.', 'No leaf storage or extension instructions are required.'),
 ('Maintenance', 'Check runners, locks, alignment and stability in both positions.', 'Inspect joints, finish and chair clearance without an extension mechanism.')],
 'A hypothetical rental sleeps six but its dining area doubles as a route to the patio. A four-seat table that extends to six seems convenient until the end chair blocks the patio door. Moving the table, reducing its width or selecting a different dining location may solve the problem more effectively than buying extra chairs.',
 ['Tape the closed and open dimensions on the floor. Place chairs or cardboard footprints where seated guests will sit.', 'Walk the routes to the kitchen, bathroom and exterior doors in each configuration. Check chair movement as well as the tabletop edge.', 'Decide who changes the table configuration. If guests do it, provide short model-specific instructions and a clear storage location.', 'Create a turnover inspection for the mechanism or fixed joints. Keep the approved seating arrangement in the property photo record.'],
 ['Are all advertised seats comfortable with full place settings?', 'Where are leaves and extra chairs stored?', 'Can one person operate the extension as instructed?']),
('blackout-curtains-vs-roller-shades', 'Blackout curtains vs roller shades for rental bedrooms',
 'Light control depends on the window, mounting method and edge gaps. Compare the installed solution in the room rather than relying on fabric names or a product image.', 'Blackout curtains', 'Blackout roller shades', [
 ('Coverage', 'Width, overlap and mounting height affect light around the panels.', 'Inside mounts may leave edge gaps; outside mounts need suitable fixing space.'),
 ('Cleaning', 'Check panel weight, care instructions and removal effort.', 'Check approved surface cleaning and whether the fabric can be replaced.'),
 ('Operation', 'Guests need accessible pulls and panels that move without snagging.', 'Choose appropriate controls and have the installed assembly checked against safety instructions.')],
 'A hypothetical east-facing bedroom has a window close to a wall corner. Wide curtain stacking space is limited, while an inside-mounted shade leaves visible light at the edges. The host tests samples and considers an outside-mounted shade with a properly fitted curtain layer. The solution comes from the opening geometry, not a universal winner.',
 ['Measure the opening, trim, wall space and obstructions. Photograph the window from inside and outside the room.', 'Confirm mounting requirements and select a suitable cordless design where available. Follow manufacturer instructions and current window-covering safety guidance.', 'Test the finished room in daylight with the treatment closed. Check side, top and bottom light gaps from the bed.', 'Document cleaning and replacement parts. Give the turnover team a simple inspection for loose fittings and damaged controls.'],
 ['Will the treatment obstruct an opening or required exit?', 'What mounting surface is available?', 'How are damaged brackets, panels or shade assemblies replaced?']),
('ceiling-fan-vs-portable-fan', 'Ceiling fans vs portable fans in vacation rentals',
 'Fan selection is a comfort and placement decision. Compare installation needs, controls, storage and the route guests use around the room. Neither option resolves an underlying heating or cooling defect.', 'Installed ceiling fan', 'Portable fan', [
 ('Installation', 'Requires an appropriate mounting arrangement and qualified installation where needed.', 'Requires an appropriate power location and stable placement following its instructions.'),
 ('Guest control', 'Provide an intuitive wall control or a clearly labeled remote.', 'Guests can reposition it within the safe placement limits.'),
 ('Turnover', 'Include blade cleaning, control checks and inspection for unusual movement.', 'Include guard cleaning, cable checks, storage and return to its designated position.')],
 'A hypothetical top-floor rental gets warm in one bedroom. A portable fan offers a quick comfort option, but its proposed location places a cable across the route to the bathroom. An installed fan may free floor space if the ceiling and mounting allow it. The host should also investigate why that room differs from the others.',
 ['Record the room dimensions, ceiling features and existing controls. Note where guests sleep, dress and walk at night.', 'Compare suitable models using actual installation and operating instructions. For ceiling work, obtain a qualified assessment of mounting and electrical requirements.', 'Test every control from the guest perspective. Label remotes and identify who replaces batteries or misplaced controls.', 'Add cleaning and condition checks to the property checklist. Keep the fan reachable for maintenance without expecting cleaners to improvise equipment.'],
 ['Is the chosen model suitable for this location?', 'Who checks the mounting or portable base?', 'What happens when the remote or control fails?']),
('modular-vs-one-piece-sofa', 'Modular vs one-piece sofas for short-term rentals',
 'A sofa must reach the living room, stay in position and fit the guest layout. Compare delivery geometry and the connection system alongside seat count and appearance.', 'Modular sofa', 'One-piece sofa', [
 ('Access', 'Smaller sections may navigate tight turns, but each section still needs measurements.', 'The complete frame must pass the entire delivery route.'),
 ('Layout', 'Can support different arrangements if connectors and room dimensions permit.', 'Provides a fixed arrangement with fewer separate sections to realign.'),
 ('Repair', 'Ask if individual sections, connectors and covers can be purchased later.', 'Ask about cushion, leg and upholstery repair options for the whole unit.')],
 'A hypothetical apartment has a narrow entrance followed by a sharp hallway turn. A one-piece sofa fits the room but cannot make the turn. A modular alternative solves access only if its largest section fits and the connected arrangement preserves the balcony route. Smaller boxes do not excuse skipping the floor plan.',
 ['Measure the complete route including lift, stairs, doors and turns. Compare packed and unpacked dimensions and the delivery company’s requirements.', 'Draw the assembled sofa and any alternate arrangement. Confirm how modules connect and whether guests can unintentionally separate them.', 'Request the model-specific spare-parts policy. Record part numbers and avoid assuming a future collection will use compatible connectors.', 'Install, connect and test the finished arrangement before photography. Show the cleaner the intended layout and how to recognize a loose connection.'],
 ['What is the largest indivisible section?', 'Are connectors supplied and replaceable?', 'Can the agreed arrangement remain stable on the actual floor?']),
('open-shelves-vs-closed-storage', 'Open shelving vs closed storage in vacation rentals',
 'Guest visibility and housekeeping effort pull storage decisions in different directions. Start with the inventory and decide which items guests should find immediately and which need a protected home.', 'Open shelves', 'Closed cabinets', [
 ('Discovery', 'Makes everyday items visible when labels and grouping are clear.', 'Needs intuitive placement or simple labels so guests can find supplies.'),
 ('Cleaning', 'Exposed surfaces and objects need regular dust and grease checks.', 'Doors reduce direct exposure but hinges, interiors and handles still need cleaning.'),
 ('Inventory', 'Missing items may be easy to spot against a reference photograph.', 'Use a cabinet map and inventory rather than relying on visual inspection from the doorway.')],
 'A hypothetical kitchen displays every mug, serving bowl and decorative object on open shelves. Turnovers become slow because cleaners move all the objects to wipe each surface. Keeping frequently used mugs visible and moving seldom-used serving pieces into cabinets reduces the reset work while preserving easy guest discovery.',
 ['List the items to store and how often guests use each one. Separate guest inventory from owner supplies and cleaning products.', 'Assign a reachable location appropriate to the item. Confirm shelf fixing, load limits and any anchoring requirements using the product instructions.', 'Create a photograph or map showing the finished arrangement. Limit decorative objects to those the turnover team can realistically maintain.', 'Observe a complete reset and revise the layout where guests or cleaners must move too many items. Review after actual use rather than adding more storage automatically.'],
 ['Which items need to be visible?', 'Can the shelf be cleaned without moving fragile objects overhead?', 'How are missing or damaged items recorded?']),
('individual-vs-bulk-furniture-delivery', 'Individual deliveries vs consolidated furniture receiving',
 'The delivery method determines who discovers damage, protects goods and gets everything to the installation site. Compare receiving responsibilities before calculating any freight saving.', 'Direct individual deliveries', 'Consolidated receiving', [
 ('Scheduling', 'Multiple carriers need site access, tracking and a person available to receive.', 'Goods can be grouped for installation after reaching the receiving location.'),
 ('Inspection', 'Inspect each arrival within the seller’s reporting window.', 'Specify whether the receiver opens cartons, photographs damage and files claims.'),
 ('Cost', 'Include repeated trips, missed appointments and any site storage.', 'Include receiving, inspection, storage, redelivery and handling charges.')],
 'A hypothetical remote owner orders from eight vendors for a mountain property. Direct shipping produces appointments across three weeks, but the local contact is only available on Fridays. Consolidated receiving may create a manageable final delivery, provided someone checks the goods promptly and the storage fees are understood.',
 ['Build an order tracker with vendor, item, quantity, tracking, estimated arrival and return deadlines. Name the responsible receiver for every line.', 'Request a receiving agreement specifying inspection depth, damage evidence, storage conditions and claims responsibility.', 'Confirm final-mile access and installation readiness before releasing the consolidated load. A full truck arriving before flooring work ends creates a storage problem.', 'Reconcile the delivered inventory against the approved plan. Keep packaging until inspection and claim requirements are satisfied.'],
 ['When does each return or damage-reporting window begin?', 'Who files and follows up on freight claims?', 'How are partial shipments and missing cartons handled?']),
('refresh-before-photos-vs-after-photos', 'Furnish before photography vs update photos after a refresh',
 'Photography should show the guest experience that will actually be delivered. Sequence purchasing, installation and images around what is ready, rather than treating the photo date as independent of the project.', 'Complete refresh before photography', 'Stage the refresh and re-photograph later', [
 ('Listing consistency', 'Creates a coherent record of the finished rooms and amenities.', 'Requires a clear process for retiring images of changed or removed features.'),
 ('Scheduling', 'Needs the important items installed, inspected and cleaned before the shoot.', 'Can spread work across available dates but may require repeat photographer visits.'),
 ('Budget', 'Compare the complete shoot and styling cost once the property is ready.', 'Include return visits, editing, rescheduling and work to update listing image sets.')],
 'A hypothetical rental is ready except for a delayed dining table. Photographing a borrowed oversized table may produce a misleading image and require a second visit. Waiting, shooting only finished rooms or arranging a later targeted reshoot are different options; the host should select one deliberately and avoid publishing an experience that will not exist.',
 ['Create a photo-readiness list by room. Check installed lighting, final furniture, textiles and amenities rather than only delivery status.', 'Ask the photographer what a partial shoot and return visit would cost and how rescheduling works.', 'Use a room-by-room shot list showing the actual guest setup. Remove temporary staging items that imply unavailable amenities.', 'After a change, replace affected images everywhere they appear. Keep a dated image inventory so older versions are not reintroduced by another channel.'],
 ['Which unresolved items would materially change the images?', 'What is the return-visit fee?', 'Who updates every booking channel after the reshoot?']),
('theme-room-vs-flexible-guest-room', 'Themed bedrooms vs flexible guest bedrooms in Airbnb',
 'A themed room can create a strong visual identity, while a flexible room can serve more guest configurations. Compare the target stay and replacement burden before committing to built-in features or specialized decor.', 'Strongly themed bedroom', 'Flexible guest bedroom', [
 ('Guest fit', 'Works when the concept serves the intended guests and still provides comfortable basics.', 'Supports changing guest needs through adaptable furniture and restrained decoration.'),
 ('Replacement', 'Bespoke pieces may need custom repairs or a backup supplier.', 'Repeatable items can simplify purchasing if exact specifications are recorded.'),
 ('Evolution', 'Major changes may require repainting, wall repair or replacement of fixed features.', 'Art, textiles and portable accents can change without rebuilding the room.')],
 'A hypothetical family rental considers a heavily themed bedroom with fixed decorative structures. Another plan uses the same color story through art, bedding and removable accessories. If the host is uncertain about the eventual guest mix, the second plan preserves more options while allowing a coherent photographic identity.',
 ['Write a guest brief describing who uses the room, their luggage, sleeping arrangement and practical needs. Do not substitute a mood board for that brief.', 'Separate reversible decoration from permanent construction. Obtain appropriate permission and qualified input before altering fixed elements.', 'Cost replacement and cleaning for unusual items. Check whether the concept relies on branding, artwork or imagery you have permission to use.', 'Review the room from bed height and at night. Confirm lighting, charging, storage and circulation still work when decorative features are present.'],
 ['Which parts of the concept can be replaced locally?', 'Can the room serve a different guest group later?', 'Does every decorative object justify its cleaning and repair effort?']),
('identical-vs-unique-multi-property-design', 'Identical vs unique furnishing across multiple rentals',
 'A portfolio can standardize operational parts without making every property look identical. Compare what should be repeatable for purchasing and maintenance with what should respond to each floor plan and guest brief.', 'Standardized furniture specification', 'Distinct property-by-property plan', [
 ('Operations', 'Shared SKUs can simplify spare inventory, care instructions and reordering.', 'Different products require separate care records and replacement planning.'),
 ('Fit', 'Every standard item must still fit the specific room and delivery route.', 'Dimensions can respond to the building instead of forcing a standard package.'),
 ('Identity', 'Use adaptable finishes and art to avoid relying on a single fixed visual formula.', 'Keep practical standards for comfort and turnover even when the visual style changes.')],
 'A hypothetical owner has three houses with different bedroom widths. One bed and nightstand combination fits two houses but crowds the third. Standardizing mattress protection, linen labeling and lamp controls may still help, while selecting a narrower nightstand for the third property respects its actual geometry.',
 ['Build a shared specification for items that do not depend heavily on room dimensions. Record approved alternatives and supplier references.', 'Audit each property for fit and access before placing repeat orders. Do not copy a previous cart without checking quantities and clearances.', 'Define which visual elements can vary: art, accent color, textiles or selected statement furniture. Keep care records tied to actual products.', 'Track failures by property and SKU. Use those records to improve the next purchasing decision rather than assuming standardization itself creates durability.'],
 ['Which items are genuinely interchangeable?', 'Who approves a substitution across the portfolio?', 'How are care instructions kept accurate when models change?']),
('repairable-vs-lowest-price-furniture', 'Repairable furniture vs the lowest purchase price for rentals',
 'A low price can be sensible for a lightly used item, but frequent-use furniture deserves a replacement plan. Compare the installed cost and the consequences of one component failing, using realistic assumptions rather than promised lifespan.', 'Furniture with replaceable components', 'Lowest-price acceptable option', [
 ('Evidence', 'Request a parts list, care instructions and actual repair availability.', 'Check the same structural and care information instead of assuming price reveals quality.'),
 ('Failure response', 'A damaged leg or cover may be replaced if the part is still available.', 'The whole item may need replacement when components cannot be sourced.'),
 ('Total cost', 'Include repair labor, freight, spare parts and any temporary substitute.', 'Include disposal, repeat assembly, delivery and room reset after replacement.')],
 'A hypothetical rental compares two dining chairs. One costs more and has an orderable seat and hardware; the other has no spare-parts process. If a seat fails, the first may be easier to return to service. That advantage disappears if the spare takes months to arrive and no backup chair exists. Availability and response time matter alongside repairability.',
 ['Choose the failure you are planning for: stained cover, damaged leg, loose hardware or broken drawer. Ask how the supplier handles that exact issue.', 'Make two cost scenarios using your own quotes. Include the first purchase and one repair or complete replacement; do not invent a service-life guarantee.', 'Check component stock and lead times, then decide whether to hold a spare or a substitute item on site.', 'Record model identifiers and purchase dates. Reassess after real maintenance events and replace assumptions with the costs you actually incurred.'],
 ['Can the vulnerable component be purchased separately today?', 'Is repair practical with the local team available?', 'What is the fallback when the room must be ready tomorrow?']),
]
